  "narrative": "In the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continue to thrive, building upon the rich heritage of their ancestors. The natural hair movement remains a powerful force, encouraging individuals to celebrate their kinky textures and reject Eurocentric beauty standards. Styles like afros, cornrows, and box braids are staples in many communities, not only as protective styles but also as expressions of identity and cultural pride. Dreadlocks, too, continue to be a popular choice for those who seek to honor their African roots. While the Jheri curl may have been a fleeting trend in the past, its legacy lives on in the resilience and creativity of Black women's hair culture. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, but one thing is clear: the 2020s are a time of unapologetic self-expression and celebration of African diasporic beauty.",
